Q: Is there any data structure that behaves like both a Set and Map in OOP? A: <h2>Use a Map&lt;Integer, Person&gt; and never duplicate the key</h2>
<p>Make the map key the single source of truth, and ensure the Person’s citizenNumber is consistent (or even remove it from the object if you can compute or pass it only through the map).</p>
<p>Keep citizenNumber in Person but enforce consistency on put</p>
<p>Wrap the Map in a small repository/DAO that inserts by the person’s own key:</p>
<pre><code>final class Person {
    private final int citizenNumber; // make it final to keep it immutable
    private final int age;
    private final String name;

    public Person(int citizenNumber, int age, String name) {
        this.citizenNumber = citizenNumber;
        this.age = age;
        this.name = name;
    }
    public int getCitizenNumber() { return citizenNumber; }
    public int getAge() { return age; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
}

final class People {
    private final Map&lt;Integer, Person&gt; byCitizen = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();

    public Person put(Person p) {
        // No mismatch possible: the key always comes from the person.
        return byCitizen.put(p.getCitizenNumber(), p);
    }

    public Person get(int citizenNumber) {
        return byCitizen.get(citizenNumber);
    }

    public boolean containsCitizen(int citizenNumber) {
        return byCitizen.containsKey(citizenNumber);
    }

    public Person remove(int citizenNumber) {
        return byCitizen.remove(citizenNumber);
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Print variables not found in &quot;unless -e&quot; statement A: <p>This will show you all files that are missing, NOT only first one missing,</p>
<pre><code>@$_ and die &quot;Files missing: @$_\n&quot; 
  for [ grep { !-e } $FileOne, $Filetwo, $FileThree ];
</code></pre>

Q: How to apply Bean Validation constraints after JSON Patch in Spring Boot? A: <p>Right now you do:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Product product = objectMapper.treeToValue(afterChanges, Product.class);
</code></pre>
<p>But you never pass product to Spring’s validation infrastructure. Spring does not automatically validate arbitrary objects created in your service method bodies. As such, the <code>@NotBlank</code> and <code>@Positive</code> constraints you defined in the <code>Product</code> class won't run post-patch.</p>
<p>You can trigger validation for the patched product by manually invoking validation with a <code>Validator</code>, e.g by injecting a Spring <code>jakarta.validation.Validator</code> and calling it after applying the patch. An example is shown below:</p>
<pre><code>import jakarta.validation.ConstraintViolation;
import jakarta.validation.Validator;
import jakarta.validation.ConstraintViolationException;

@Service
public class ProductService {

    private final Validator validator;
    private final ObjectMapper objectMapper;

    public ProductService(Validator validator, ObjectMapper objectMapper) {
        this.validator = validator;
        this.objectMapper = objectMapper;
    }

    public ProductResponse updateProduct(String productId, JsonPatch jsonPatch)
            throws JsonPatchException, JsonProcessingException {
        Product existingProduct = findProductById(productId);
        JsonNode patchedProduct = jsonPatch.apply(objectMapper.valueToTree(existingProduct));
        Product updatedProduct = objectMapper.treeToValue(patchedProduct, Product.class);

        Set&lt;ConstraintViolation&lt;Product&gt;&gt; violations = validator.validate(updatedProduct);
        if (!violations.isEmpty()) {
            throw new ConstraintViolationException(violations);
        }

        return productMapper.productToProductResponse(updatedProduct);
    }
}
</code></pre>
